result.reasoning:
The scratchpad review files do not exist, so I checked the finding against draft 4 directly. The core of the finding holds.

Line 323 ("Where the case has no probe, the code is implementation defect or unclear.") is a sibling bullet under Burden. It is not nested under the probe-type bullet on line 322, so nothing in the text limits it to probe-type alternatives. Step 2b only probes:
- extraction-lost cases, except `admitted:ignored`;
- routing-lost rows;
- `key_reached` losses whose holding key was pruned out.

Read literally, then, every other case is forced to implementation defect or unclear. That covers:
- A0/A1 state-rule cases, so architectural weakness and trade-off become impossible at deterministic boundaries;
- A2 support-check cases, which line 317 names explicitly but which can never have a probe;
- the WD rule's architectural branch (line 338, D4 text), which involves no 2b probe;
- gatekeeper `admitted:ignored` cases;
- reading cases whose holding key was in the question-routing prompt;
- conversational-difficulty and evaluation-defect codes for any unprobed case.

This is a pre-registered codebook. A model pre-coder works from it "in a fresh context", and V4 codes blind from it, so a literal reading is the one the protocol has to survive. A one-line scoping fix is worth making.

Two parts of the finding are weaker:
- **Point (1).** Even with 323 scoped, line 322 alone already makes design-or-model unreachable at extraction and routing when Step 2b is not run, because their probe-type alternatives cannot be ruled out. So "any case if Step 2b is not run" is mostly dead text. It is not only 323 that blocks it.
- **The proposed line 322 append is too broad.** "Offline check showing the item was present (resolver or question-time)" would rule out unpruned resolution for routing. But (ii-r) runs on every routing-lost row whatever the offline check shows. A case where the fitting key was present and (ii-r) still returns recovered would then be both ruled out and recovered under the line 262 mapping. The carve-out is needed only for probe (iii), which runs only when the key was pruned.

The proposed line 317 wording is also off. It ties A2's support check to Step 2b not being run, and it restates the Burden rule inside the class table.

